To create a link for every post on your website, you'll need to modify the nginx configuration to append a link to every HTML file in the `/usr/share/nginx/html` directory.

You can add the following configuration to your `location /` block:
```nginx
location / {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index index.html index.htm;
    try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;

    # Add link to every HTML file
    if ($uri ~* "\.html$") {
        add_header Link "<https://www.askfundu.com/post/$uri>";
    }
}
```
This configuration uses an `if` statement to check if the URL matches a HTML file (`\.html$` regular expression). If it does, it adds an `Link` header to the response with a link to the post (in this case, `https://www.askfundu.com/post/$uri`).

You'll also need to create a new server block that handles requests to `/post/<post_id>`:
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
    server_name www.askfundu.com;

    root /usr/share/nginx/html;

    location /post {
        rewrite ^/post/(.*) /show/$1 break;
    }

    location /show {
        rewrite ^(/show/)(.*) /$2 break;
    }

    location ~ ^/show/([^.]+)\.html$ {
        set $post_id $1;
        root /usr/share/nginx/html;
        internal;
    }
}
```
This configuration uses two `rewrite` blocks to redirect requests from `/post/<post_id>` to `/show/<post_id>.html`. The `location ~` block then serves the `post.html` file and sets the `$post_id` variable based on the request URL.

When a user requests a post, the `Link` header will be added to the response, pointing to the original post URL. When a user requests the original post URL, the server will redirect them to the `/show/<post_id>.html` file.

Note that this configuration assumes that your post files are named `<post_id>.html` and are stored in the `/usr/share/nginx/html` directory. You may need to modify the configuration to match your specific file structure. Additionally, you'll need to create a template for your post.html file that includes links to other posts.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

